@page
@model SimulationViewer.Pages.IndexModel

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>SMS Configuration</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/site.css" />
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Super Mendelian Sandbox &mdash; FFER Drive Configuration</h1>

    <div class="config-panel">

        <div class="config-section">
            <h3>Population &amp; Release</h3>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="generations">Generations</label>
                <input type="range" id="generations" min="5" max="100" step="1" value="30" />
                <span class="param-value" id="generations-val">30</span>
            </div>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="releaseNumber">Release males</label>
                <input type="range" id="releaseNumber" min="1" max="500" step="1" value="100" />
                <span class="param-value" id="releaseNumber-val">100</span>
            </div>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="mortality">Mortality rate</label>
                <input type="range" id="mortality" min="0.1" max="0.9" step="0.01" value="0.10" />
                <span class="param-value" id="mortality-val">0.10</span>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="config-section">
            <h3>Reproduction</h3>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="eggsPerFemale">Eggs per female</label>
                <input type="range" id="eggsPerFemale" min="1" max="200" step="1" value="50" />
                <span class="param-value" id="eggsPerFemale-val">50</span>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="config-section">
            <h3>Gene Drive Parameters</h3>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="cas9Activity">Cas9 nuclease activity</label>
                <input type="range" id="cas9Activity" min="0" max="1" step="0.01" value="0.97" />
                <span class="param-value" id="cas9Activity-val">0.97</span>
            </div>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="hdrRate">HDR rate</label>
                <input type="range" id="hdrRate" min="0" max="1" step="0.01" value="0.97" />
                <span class="param-value" id="hdrRate-val">0.97</span>
            </div>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="maternalCas9">Maternal Cas9 deposition</label>
                <input type="range" id="maternalCas9" min="0" max="1" step="0.01" value="0.10" />
                <span class="param-value" id="maternalCas9-val">0.10</span>
            </div>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="conservation">Conservation level</label>
                <input type="range" id="conservation" min="0.9" max="0.99999" step="0.00001" value="0.999" />
                <span class="param-value" id="conservation-val">0.99900</span>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="config-section">
            <h3>Migration</h3>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="migrationBaseRate">Base migration rate</label>
                <input type="range" id="migrationBaseRate" min="0" max="0.5" step="0.001" value="0.100" />
                <span class="param-value" id="migrationBaseRate-val">0.100</span>
            </div>
            <div class="migration-detail" id="migration-detail"></div>
        </div>

    </div>

    <div class="action-bar">
        <button id="btn-run" class="btn btn-run" onclick="runSimulation()">Run Simulation</button>
        <a id="btn-view" href="/Viewer" class="btn btn-view disabled">View Results</a>
    </div>

    <div id="progress-container" class="progress-container" style="display:none">
        <div class="progress-bar">
            <div class="progress-fill" id="progress-fill"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="progress-text" id="progress-text"></div>
    </div>

    <script src="/js/configure.js?v=2"></script>

    <div class="info-panel">

        <h2>About Female Fertility Gene Drives</h2>

        <p>
            A <strong>female fertility gene drive</strong> is a CRISPR-based homing construct
            inserted into a gene essential for female reproduction. In heterozygous drive
            carriers, Cas9 and a guide RNA (gRNA) cut the wild-type allele on the homologous
            chromosome during meiosis. Homology-directed repair (HDR) then copies the transgene
            onto the cut chromosome, converting heterozygotes into homozygotes and causing the
            drive to be inherited by a majority of offspring rather than the Mendelian 50%.
        </p>
        <p>
            Females homozygous for the transgene are sterile because the target gene
            is disrupted on both copies. Males are unaffected regardless of genotype. As the
            drive spreads through a population, an increasing fraction of females become
            homozygous and fail to reproduce, reducing the population's reproductive output.
            With sufficiently high drive efficiency and low resistance allele formation, this
            genetic load can cause population suppression or collapse.
        </p>
        <p>
            This strategy was first demonstrated in <em>Anopheles gambiae</em> mosquitoes by
            Hammond et al. (2016, <em>Nature Biotechnology</em>), who built drives targeting
            three female fertility genes and showed super-Mendelian inheritance rates above
            90%. Kyrou et al. (2018, <em>Nature Biotechnology</em>) subsequently targeted the
            sex determination gene <em>doublesex</em> and achieved complete population
            collapse in caged populations within 7&ndash;11 generations. More recent
            work by Hammond et al. (2021, <em>Nature Communications</em>) confirmed
            drive performance in small and large cage trials, and Simoni et al. (2020,
            <em>Nature Biotechnology</em>) demonstrated population suppression with
            a doublesex-targeting drive in large cages mimicking more natural conditions.
        </p>

        <h2>Parameter Guide</h2>

        <h3>Generations</h3>
        <p>
            The number of discrete, non-overlapping generations to simulate (5&ndash;100).
            Each generation, all adults reproduce and die. Short runs (10&ndash;20) are
            useful for testing; longer runs (50&ndash;100) reveal whether the drive
            achieves population suppression or whether resistance evolves to block it.
        </p>

        <h3>Release Males</h3>
        <p>
            The number of gene drive males released into Population 1 at generation 3
            (range: 1&ndash;500). Each release male is heterozygous for the FFER transgene
            (one drive copy, one wild-type copy). Larger releases increase the initial
            drive frequency and speed up spread, but even small releases can lead to
            population suppression if drive efficiency is high enough. Since the population
            cap is 1,000, a release of 100 represents a 10% ratio relative to resident
            population size.
        </p>

        <h3>Mortality Rate</h3>
        <p>
            Per-generation background mortality (0.1&ndash;0.9). This controls mate-finding
            difficulty: the effective number of mate-search attempts per female is
            (1 &minus; mortality) &times; carrying capacity. Higher mortality means fewer
            search attempts, so some females may fail to find a mate and not reproduce.
            At low mortality (0.1), nearly all females mate successfully; at high
            mortality (0.9), reproductive output drops sharply even without the drive.
        </p>

        <h3>Eggs per Female</h3>
        <p>
            Base fecundity: the number of eggs produced per successful mating (1&ndash;200).
            Higher values mean greater reproductive surplus over the carrying capacity,
            making the population more resilient to suppression. With 50 eggs and a cap of
            1,000, there is a large reproductive buffer; reducing this to 5&ndash;10 eggs
            makes the population much more vulnerable to the fitness load imposed by the
            drive.
        </p>

        <h3>Cas9 Nuclease Activity</h3>
        <p>
            Probability (0&ndash;1) that Cas9 successfully cuts the wild-type target allele
            during meiosis in the germline. This is used for both male and female germline
            cutting (Cas9_male and Cas9_female). At 0.97, nearly every wild-type allele
            opposite a drive allele gets cut. Lower values reduce drive efficiency and
            slow spread. At 0, the drive behaves as a normal Mendelian allele.
        </p>

        <h3>HDR Rate</h3>
        <p>
            Probability (0&ndash;1) that a Cas9-induced double-strand break is repaired by
            homology-directed repair, copying the transgene onto the cut chromosome. The
            remainder (1 &minus; HDR) is repaired by non-homologous end joining (NHEJ),
            producing resistance alleles (R1 or R2). High HDR (0.95&ndash;0.99) is essential
            for drive spread; lower values generate more resistance alleles that can block
            the drive.
        </p>

        <h3>Maternal Cas9 Deposition</h3>
        <p>
            Probability (0&ndash;1) that maternally-deposited Cas9 protein acts on the
            embryo's chromosomes after fertilization (zygotic activity). Unlike germline
            activity, zygotic cutting has a 99% reduction in HDR efficiency, meaning
            almost all zygotic cuts produce resistance alleles via NHEJ rather than
            drive copies. High maternal deposition therefore <em>accelerates resistance
            formation</em> rather than aiding drive spread. Setting this low (0.1) limits
            unwanted resistance generation; setting it high increases R2 allele frequency.
        </p>

        <h3>Conservation Level</h3>
        <p>
            Probability (0.9&ndash;0.99999) that NHEJ repair at the target site produces
            an R2 (non-functional, loss-of-function) allele rather than an R1 (functional
            resistance) allele. R2 alleles disrupt the target gene just like the transgene,
            so R2 homozygous females are also sterile &mdash; R2 effectively aids
            suppression. R1 alleles restore gene function while being resistant to
            further cutting, so R1 can halt drive spread.
            At 0.999, nearly all NHEJ produces R2 (favourable for suppression). Lower
            conservation means more R1, which can rescue the population. This parameter
            reflects the evolutionary constraint on the target site: highly conserved
            sequences tolerate fewer functional repair outcomes.
        </p>

        <h3>Base Migration Rate</h3>
        <p>
            Per-generation probability (0&ndash;0.5) that any individual migrates between
            adjacent populations. The rate decays by 10&times; for each successive pair:
            Pop 1&harr;2 gets the base rate, Pop 2&harr;3 gets base/10, Pop 3&harr;4
            gets base/100, and Pop 4&harr;5 gets base/1000. This models a linear
            geographic chain with decreasing connectivity. Higher base rates mean faster
            spatial spread of the drive; at 0, populations are completely isolated.
        </p>

        <h2>Fixed Model Assumptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Population carrying capacity:</strong> 1,000 individuals per population. Egg production above this cap is culled by density-dependent regulation (random selection of survivors).</li>
            <li><strong>Number of populations:</strong> 5, arranged in a linear chain.</li>
            <li><strong>Migration topology:</strong> Linear chain only (1&harr;2&harr;3&harr;4&harr;5). No long-range migration.</li>
            <li><strong>Migration decay:</strong> 10&times; per step (not configurable).</li>
            <li><strong>Replicates:</strong> 3 independent iterations per simulation run, each starting from a fresh wild-type population.</li>
            <li><strong>Release timing:</strong> Gene drive males are released once, at generation 3, into Population 1 only.</li>
            <li><strong>Sex determination:</strong> XY system with a Maleness-on-Y (MoY) factor. Females are XX; males are XY. Sex is determined by the presence of MoY plus maternal TRA mRNA provision.</li>
            <li><strong>Non-overlapping generations:</strong> All adults die after reproducing. There is no age structure or overlapping cohorts.</li>
            <li><strong>Mating system:</strong> Random mating within each population. Each female mates with at most one male per generation (monandry); males can mate with multiple females (polygyny).</li>
            <li><strong>Zygotic HDR reduction:</strong> 99% reduction in HDR efficiency for embryonic (post-fertilization) gene drive activity, making zygotic cutting overwhelmingly produce NHEJ resistance alleles.</li>
            <li><strong>No fitness cost of the transgene:</strong> Drive-carrying males have normal fertility and mating competitiveness. Only homozygous transgene females are sterile.</li>
            <li><strong>Drive target:</strong> FFER (female fertility) locus on autosome 2. TRA locus on autosome 3 is present but not actively targeted by the drive in this configuration.</li>
        </ul>

    </div>

</body>
</html>
